# scripts/install/EXPECTED_RELEASE — LivOS release pin (Phase 311-04, UPDSAFE-02)
#
# WHAT THIS IS
#   A single "pin line" naming the release this repo tree belongs to. update.sh's
#   livos_verify_fetched_ref() reads it out of the freshly-cloned release tree and
#   compares the resolved commit against the fetched HEAD.
#
# FORMAT (consumed by update.sh:  grep -vE '^\s*(#|$)' | head -1 | tr -d '[:space:]')
#   * Lines beginning with '#' and blank lines are ignored.
#   * The FIRST non-comment line is the pin.
#   * Use the FULL  refs/tags/<tag>  form. A refs/tags/<tag> line is resolved to a
#     commit via  git rev-parse "<tag>^{commit}"  INSIDE the cloned tree, then
#     compared to the fetched HEAD SHA. Because boxes clone a release BY that same
#     tag, HEAD == the tag's commit == what refs/tags/<tag> resolves to → a
#     self-consistent match.
#
# WARN-ONLY (Phase 311) — NOT enforcement
#   In Phase 311 a pin mismatch is WARN-ONLY: livos_verify_fetched_ref() logs a
#   SIGNATURE-WARN, records a signature_verification verdict into the update-history
#   JSON, and PROCEEDS — it never aborts an update. The fail-closed flip (refuse to
#   deploy on mismatch) is Phase 312, a strictly LATER release, gated on one full
#   release cycle of this warn-only telemetry. Do NOT add enforcement here or in the
#   verify function as part of Phase 311.
#
# CURRENT VALUE = refs/tags/v1.0.0 (FIRST STABLE RELEASE — SemVer migration. The release line moves from the legacy 2-part vNN.MM tags (…v45.30, v45.31-beta.11) to strict 3-part Semantic Versioning MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH. v1.0.0 is the first stable cut under the new scheme: bug fix → 1.0.1, new feature → 1.1.0, breaking change → 2.0.0. Cut as a STABLE, make_latest=true release. NOTE on update reachability: the box update path compares tags by STRING equality (update.ts:456 `release.tag_name !== deployedRelease`) — NOT semver — so STABLE-channel boxes on v45.30 will see v1.0.0 as an update automatically even though 1.0.0 < 45.30 numerically. BETA-channel boxes use pickMaxReleaseTag (semver.gt), which treats 1.0.0 as older than 45.31 — those (few, operator-controlled) boxes must switch to the stable channel to see v1.0.0. Encoded VM streaming verified working on Ubuntu guests; Windows-guest jitter is a known guest-OS limitation, not a pipeline defect.)
#   The pin line below names the exact tag being cut in this commit (pre-tag-prep
#   flow, step 1). release.yml verifies pin == tag and publishes. On a box the
#   warn-only path resolves it to the tag's commit and records a self-consistent
#   match. The NEXT release cut MUST bump this line again to its own tag before
#   tagging (see the mandatory procedure below); leaving a stale pin makes
#   release.yml refuse the next publish until it is bumped.
#
# MANDATORY PRE-TAG RELEASE-CUT PROCEDURE (self-consistency; never force-tag)
#   A git tag's tree is immutable once pushed, so the pin MUST already be correct
#   INSIDE the tagged commit — a post-tag "generate" step could never reach a box
#   cloning that tag. Therefore, before cutting any release tag:
#     (1) bump the pin line below to  refs/tags/<next-tag>  (the exact tag to cut);
#     (2) commit that change on master;
#     (3) THEN create and push the tag  (git tag <next-tag> ; git push origin <next-tag>).
#   This ordering guarantees the tagged commit's own tree carries a pin equal to its
#   own tag. Do NOT force-move a tag (git tag -f) — the pre-tag-prep flow makes it
#   unnecessary and it is explicitly disallowed. release.yml verifies this and
#   refuses to publish on any tag/pin mismatch.
refs/tags/v1.0.0
